+## 5. ADDENDUM 2026-08-06 — the absolute energies ARE converged; the failure is an epoch, not the ladder
+
+A parallel record (the 2026-08-06 completion note) reported `ME p = 0.54`, `KE p = 0.29`,
+`tot-E p = 0.22` and concluded *"do NOT quote a converged flux-retention number off this ladder."*
+Those orders are correct **but they are measured at t = 1.0** — the same singular stall at which
+§2 already rules the well-behaved ratio observable unusable (p = 0.21, residual 45.6 %). Read at
+one epoch, the two records look contradictory; read across time they are the same fact.
+
+`docs/validation/scripts/wp7_time_resolved_convergence.py` recomputes the observed order for the
+**absolute** observables at matched time (linear interpolation in `t`, since the three legs take
+different steps). It first reproduces §2's published `MEtor/MEpol` column — 1.21 / 1.14 / 1.15 /
+1.33 / 1.56 / 1.63 / 1.16 / 0.21 against the table's 1.21 / 1.14 / 1.14 / 1.32 / 1.54 / 1.60 /
+1.16 / 0.21 — so the estimator is the same one, not a new convention.
+
+**`p` alone is the wrong metric here, and reading it alone is what produced the pessimistic
+verdict.** `p = log₂(|Δ₁|/|Δ₂|)` is a ratio of two differences; when both are at noise level it
+returns nan, negative values, or 3.07 with no physical content. At t = 0.6–0.8 the residuals are
+0.00–0.09 %, and `p` is duly meaningless there. What bounds the discretization error is the
+Richardson estimate `err₅₁₂ ≈ |v₅₁₂ − v₂₅₆| / (2^p − 1)`, which uses residual and order together:
+
+| t | mass | ME | KE | tot-E |
+|---|---|---|---|---|
+| 0.20 | 0.01 % | 0.01 % | 0.22 % | 0.00 % |
+| 0.90 | 0.02 % | 0.00 % | 0.01 % | 0.00 % |
+| 0.95 | 0.03 % | 0.01 % | 0.09 % | 0.03 % |
+| 0.98 | 0.03 % | 0.11 % | 0.72 % | 0.36 % |
+| **1.00** | 0.03 % | **1.06 %** | **13.27 %** | **11.32 %** |
+
+(entries where `p` is unusable are reported in the script as a bare-residual lower bound, flagged)
+
+**Conclusion.** Through **t ≤ 0.95** every absolute energy is converged to **≤ 0.09 %**, and mass
+to 0.03 % across the whole run. The non-convergence is confined to the final ~2 % of the
+integration, where low `p` and a non-trivial residual combine — worst for KE at 13.3 %. The
+correct statement is therefore an **epoch restriction (read at t ≤ 0.95, as §2 already required
+for the ratio), not a rejection of the ladder.** The blanket warning is superseded.
+
+**What this does NOT license.** It does not make `mag-Jsq` quotable (§2: diverging under
+refinement, ×2.66/×2.84), it does not extend to the AMR flagship (this ladder is three *uniform*
+legs), and it says nothing about a flux-retention value measured *at* the singularity — which is
+precisely the epoch that fails. A flux-retention claim must be read at a matched pre-singular
+epoch and carry the errors above.
